{
  "id": "populating-next-right-pointers-in-each-node-ii",
  "questionId": "populating-next-right-pointers-in-each-node-ii",
  "slug": "populating-next-right-pointers-in-each-node-ii",
  "title": "Populating Next Right Pointers in Each Node II",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "'nearest node to the right on the same level' is exactly the node processed right after this one in a left-to-right level-order traversal, within the same level - it doesn't matter that the tree is irregular",
    "process the queue one level at a time; as you pop nodes off in order, each node's answer is simply the value of the node you pop right after it, unless it was the last pop of that level",
    "collect an entire level's worth of indices before wiring them together, so you know exactly which node is last on that level",
    "initialize the output array to all -101 first, so sentinel and out-of-bounds positions are correctly left untouched, and only overwrite positions that correspond to real nodes"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "assuming the tree is perfect and using the array's own index arithmetic to guess siblings, instead of tracking true level membership via BFS",
    "linking the last node of a level to the first node of the next level instead of assigning it -1",
    "forgetting that a node can have only one child (or neither), so children must be individually checked for validity rather than always enqueuing both"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a flat integer array named `tree`, using complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 can never be a genuine value\n- unlike a perfect binary tree, any node may be missing either child, both children, or neither - the shape is arbitrary\n\n**Connect every node to the nearest node to its right on the same level (there might be gaps between them, since some nodes on a level can be missing children), and build and return a new array with the exact same shape (same length, same positions) as `tree`, where each position that held a real node now holds the value of the nearest node to its right on the same level, or -1 if no such node exists.**\n\nPositions that were sentinel or missing in the input should stay exactly -101 in the output.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= length of tree <= 2000",
      "every real node value is in the range -100 to 100",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "the tree may be of any shape, not just a perfect binary tree"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree",
      "an empty tree, represented as [-101]",
      "a level where some nodes have missing children, creating a gap that the next-right connection must skip over",
      "a completely skewed tree, where most levels only have one real node"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, -101, -101, 5]",
        "output": "[-1, 3, -1, 5, -101, -101, -1]",
        "expectedOutput": "[-1, 3, -1, 5, -101, -101, -1]",
        "explanation": "1 is alone on level 0; 2's nearest right neighbor on level 1 is 3; 4's nearest right neighbor on level 2 is 5 (skipping the missing children of node 3)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "[-1, 20, -1, -101, -101, 7, -1]",
        "expectedOutput": "[-1, 20, -1, -101, -101, 7, -1]",
        "explanation": "9's nearest right neighbor is 20; 15's nearest right neighbor is 7; the two sentinel positions stay -101"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1]",
        "output": "[-1]",
        "expectedOutput": "[-1]",
        "explanation": "the only node is alone on its level"
      }
    ],
